# Copy to backend/.env and fill in. That file is gitignored; this one
# is tracked, so never put a real credential here.
#
#   cp backend/.env.example backend/.env

# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The local Postgres from the README's docker line. Schema lives in
# backend/srs/data_structure.sql:
#
#   docker run -d --name jp-db -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=dev -p 5432:5432 postgres:16
#   docker exec -i jp-db psql -U postgres -c "CREATE DATABASE jp;"
#   docker exec -i jp-db psql -U postgres -d jp < backend/srs/data_structure.sql
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:dev@localhost:5432/jp

# ── Auth, local ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this and the two Supabase values below can stay empty: every
# request is treated as this user and no token is verified. See the
# comment in core/auth.py for why this exists and why it is safe to
# ship as an unset variable.
#
# Any string works — it is the user_id every row gets keyed to, so
# keep the same one between runs or your data appears to vanish.
DEV_USER_ID=local-dev-user

# ── Auth, real ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed when DEV_USER_ID is unset. SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY is the
# service-role key: it bypasses row-level security on the whole
# project, so treat it like a database password and never paste it
# into a chat, an issue, or a commit.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=

# ── CORS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated origins allowed *in addition to* the deployed
# frontend, which is always permitted. This is where a local Vite dev
# server goes; Vite picks the first free port from 5173 upward, so
# list the ones you actually use.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173

# ── Credits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The credits economy (core/credits.py) runs in SHADOW MODE by default:
# the ledger is kept and the app prints the balance, but no review is
# refused, no pass feature is closed and no deck limit is applied. Set
# this to 1 — after the grace announcement — to enforce all three
# (402 out_of_credits / pass_required / limit_reached). Leave unset
# locally and in tests.
# CREDITS_ENFORCE=1

# ── Exam audio storage ────────────────────────────────────────
# Where study/exam_tts.py writes generated listening-section MP3s and
# main.py serves them from. One resolution, shared: main.py mounts
# exactly what exam_tts.audio_dir() returns, so the two cannot disagree.
# On Render this should be the persistent disk's mount path (render.yaml
# mounts one at /data), or generated audio is lost on the next deploy
# while the URL referencing it stays in the stored exam paper forever.
# Leave unset locally — it falls back to backend/datas/exam_audio.
#
# Setting it to a path the process cannot write to is no longer fatal:
# exam_tts.py probes the directory at startup, logs an error naming it,
# and falls back to a writable one, and a clip that has gone missing is
# re-synthesized on demand from the script stored in the paper
# (study/exam_audio_repair.py). The disk is still what makes clips
# permanent — the fallback is how the listening section stays up
# without it, not a substitute for it.
EXAM_AUDIO_DIR=

# ── Speech: the voice engine (study/voice_engine.py, plan 121) ───
# Every clip the server makes -- exam listening, dictation, and the card
# readings /api/tts falls back to -- comes from a VOICEVOX Nemo engine
# over HTTP. On Render it is the private service render.yaml declares,
# and this is filled from its `hostport` (no http:// needed). Locally,
# run the same image and point here:
#
#   docker run -d --name voicevox-nemo -p 127.0.0.1:50121:50121 \
#       voicevox/voicevox_nemo_engine:cpu-0.23.0
#
# Port 50121 -- not the 50021 of the main VOICEVOX engine, which the
# image's own README example still shows and nothing in it listens on.
#
# UNSET = no server audio, which is not fatal: listening sections are
# skipped (before any model call is paid for), dictation and /api/tts
# answer 503, and cards still speak through the device's own voice.
VOICEVOX_URL=
# The voices, by slot (the reader, A the woman, B the man, the exam
# narrator), for trying a choice out locally with
# scripts/audition_voices.py. Production voices are
# voice_engine.DEFAULT_VOICES, changed together with VOICE_REV -- never
# here: a new voice without a new revision would mix two voices in one
# clip store.
# TTS_VOICES=女声6,女声6,男声1,女声1
# Read timeout per request, seconds (default 30), and how many requests
# may be at the engine at once (default 2).
# VOICEVOX_TIMEOUT_S=30
# VOICEVOX_CONCURRENCY=2

# ── The video link ingest (study/captions.py) ─────────────────
# UNSET = the feature is dark, which is the shipped state and is not a
# broken one: a learner adds a subtitle file or uses the 字幕取り
# bookmarklet, exactly as before. Set these and the analyzer grows a
# "get the subtitles" button that takes a YouTube link on its own.
#
# What is being bought is not bandwidth, it is ADDRESSES. YouTube
# blocks datacenter IPs, which is every IP Render has on every plan, so
# the fetch only works through a ROTATING RESIDENTIAL proxy. Webshare
# sells three products and TWO OF THEM ARE USELESS HERE:
#
#   Proxy Server ($2.99, 250 GB)        datacenter. Blocked, like Render.
#   Static Residential ($6.00, 250 GB)  ISP proxies. ASN-detectable;
#                                       blocked, and a flagged address
#                                       stays flagged.
#   Rotating Residential ($3.50, 1 GB)  THE ONE TO BUY.
#
# The generous bandwidth on the first two is the trap -- gigabytes are
# cheap when the addresses do not work. 1 GB is ~2,500 analyses at
# ~0.4 MB each, and YOUTUBE_FETCH_DAILY_CAP below keeps a month inside
# it. See docs/adr/0003's 2026-09-10 amendment for the measurements.
#
# On Render these belong in the dashboard, not render.yaml: they are
# credentials.
WEBSHARE_PROXY_USERNAME=
WEBSHARE_PROXY_PASSWORD=

# An escape hatch for a different provider -- a full proxy URL,
# http://user:pass@host:port. It carries NO default about rotation, so
# whoever sets it owns that choice; get it wrong and every fetch is
# blocked exactly as it was before any of this existed.
# YOUTUBE_HTTP_PROXY=

# The spend ceiling, per day. Metered bandwidth means an unbounded
# retry is a bill rather than an outage, so this is enforced in code:
# over the cap the API refuses at the door (429) and names the file
# path, instead of quietly spending. Defaults are 60 a day across the
# app and 10 per learner.
# YOUTUBE_FETCH_DAILY_CAP=60
# YOUTUBE_FETCH_DAILY_CAP_PER_USER=10

# ── LLM providers ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exam generation, reading comprehension and phrase analysis all go
# through study/llm_shared.py's chat(), which tries providers in order
# and falls over to the next one when an account is out. Set at least
# one key; anything left empty is dropped from the list rather than
# tried and failed.
#
# PAID FIRST, FREE LAST. The two free tiers at the bottom of this
# section cannot carry a commercial deployment — NVIDIA's hosted
# catalogue is licensed for prototyping (production is NVIDIA AI
# Enterprise) and OpenRouter's `:free` models cap at 1,000 requests a
# DAY across the whole account, which is about 28 exam papers. They are
# kept as a degradation path, not as the primary. See
# docs/llm-commercial-plan.md.
#
# Google AI Studio (https://aistudio.google.com/apikey) — the primary.
# Reached through Google's OpenAI-compatibility endpoint, so it is an
# ordinary entry in study/llm_shared.py's catalogue. GEMINI_API_KEY is
# read as an alias, since that is what Google's own docs export.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Model ids. UNVERIFIED defaults: they were chosen on published pricing
# without an account to confirm them against, which is not how any other
# model id in this project was adopted. The FIRST thing to do with a new
# key is:
#
#   python -m scripts.check_llm_models          # do the ids exist?
#   python -m scripts.check_llm_models --smoke  # do they write Japanese?
#   python -m scripts.check_llm_models --vision # can they read tategaki?
#
# GOOGLE_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite
# GOOGLE_VISION_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash

# OpenAI (https://platform.openai.com) — the paid fallback, so a single
# provider outage is not an outage. Key starts with "sk-".
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5-mini
# OPENAI_CHEAP_MODEL=gpt-5-nano

# NVIDIA NIM (https://build.nvidia.com) — OpenAI-compatible, key starts
# with "nvapi-". Free tier at time of writing: 1,000 inference credits
# (+4,000 on request), 40 requests/minute, phone verification required,
# and explicitly NO production SLA. DEVELOPMENT ONLY — production use
# needs an NVIDIA AI Enterprise licence, which is why it is no longer in
# the default provider order. Name it in LLM_PROVIDER_ORDER to use it.
#
# Both spellings are read, because the key was first added under the
# misspelled name and silently ignoring a key that is right there is
# worse than accepting two names. Either works.
NVDIA_API_KEY=
# NVIDIA_API_KEY=

# OpenRouter (https://openrouter.ai) — key starts with "sk-or-".
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Which providers to try, in order. Defaults to
# "google,openai,openrouter" — paid first, the free tier last as a
# degradation path. Name a single provider to pin generation to it, or
# put nvidia back in front for local work:
# LLM_PROVIDER_ORDER=nvidia,openrouter

# Overrides only the FIRST model in the OpenRouter list. Leave unset
# unless you are deliberately pinning one -- a stale value here once
# pointed at a model that had moved off the free tier, and every call
# paid a 404 for it. Verify any change with:
#
#   python -m scripts.check_llm_models          # do the ids still exist?
#   python -m scripts.check_llm_models --smoke  # do they still write Japanese?
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=

# ── Photo input (OCR) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Images a learner may send to the vision model in one day. Counted per
# learner in ocr_usage, on THEIR local day (user_profiles.tz_offset_min),
# and a spent allowance is a 429 the screen has its own message for.
#
# It protects the bill: every image is billed since the app went paid,
# at roughly 1,400 input tokens plus a few hundred out. The default
# covers a manga chapter photographed page by page and still bounds one
# learner's worst month. Raise it deliberately, not by reflex.
# OCR_DAILY_LIMIT=20

# ── Reading comprehension ─────────────────────────────────────
# NEW exercises a learner may have generated in one day. Reading from
# the shared pool is free and is NOT counted -- an exercise served from
# comprehension_pool was paid for by whoever read it first -- so this
# only meets someone who outruns the pool, and they are handed a text
# they have read before rather than refused. Counted on the learner's
# own day, like the OCR limit.
#
# One generation is up to three model calls and is the most expensive
# thing this app does. The default is about two hours of reading
# practice a day; nobody reaches it by studying.
# COMPREHENSION_DAILY_LIMIT=10

# ── Composition (作文) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Tutor reviews of a learner's own sentences in one day (plan 125).
# Counted per learner in daily_usage, feature "composition", on their
# local day like the two above. Past it the run goes on -- the local
# check still says whether the point is in the sentence and the learner
# still grades -- only the tutor is done for the day, and the screen
# says so.
#
# One review is one call, the learner's sentence in the prompt (so
# nothing is shared) under a stable system block (so the prefix is
# cached). The default is a long session twice over; the right number
# is a measurement nobody has yet, and daily_usage holds the data.
# COMPOSITION_DAILY_LIMIT=30

# ── What each call costs ──────────────────────────────────────
# study/llm_shared.py logs one line per billed response to the logger
# "study.llm_shared.usage", carrying the task, the model, and the token
# counts the provider reported:
#
#   llm-usage task=comprehension provider=google model=gemini-3.1-flash-lite \
#             in=2731 out=2984 cached=1800 reasoning=0 ms=4210 ok=1
#
# Nothing has to be configured for it. To total a month of it:
#
#   grep llm-usage app.log | python -m scripts.llm_usage_report
